<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick Lugard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lugard</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick Lugard was a British colonial administrator who became high commissioner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-nigeria-protectorate">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a> in 1900. His forces conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> between 1900 and 1903, after which he governed through the existing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> in a system known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard.jpg" alt="Frederick Lugard" width="283" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elliott &amp; Fry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LordLugard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick John Dealtry Lugard was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> on 22 January 1858, the son of an army chaplain, and was brought up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a>. He was educated at Rossall School and Sandhurst and was commissioned into the 9th Foot in 1878. He served in the Second Anglo-Afghan War, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> campaign of 1884 to 1885 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-burmese-war">Third Anglo-Burmese War</a>, and won the Distinguished Service Order in 1887.</p><p>After a failed attachment to a divorcee, for whom he left his post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, he made a fresh start in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, where he led two unsuccessful expeditions in 1888 and 1889 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> trader Mlozi in the Karonga War near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1889 Lugard joined the Imperial British East Africa Company and from 1890 to 1892 was military administrator of Uganda. He preferred blood brotherhood ceremonies to written treaties, and made one with Waiyaki Wa Hinga at Dagoretti in 1890. In 1894 he secured treaties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgu">Borgu</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-niger-company">Royal Niger Company</a> and reached Nikki weeks before a French rival, though Nikki was later integrated into French Dahomey.</p><p>He organised the West African Frontier Force in 1897. On 1 January 1900 he proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-nigeria-protectorate">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a> as its first high commissioner. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-of-sokoto">Sultan of Sokoto</a> and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a> princes had refused to meet their treaty obligations, and a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> in 1903 brought the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> under British control. He stopped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a>, abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, and reorganised taxation. He was knighted in 1901 and resigned in 1906.</p><p>He then served as Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> from 1907 to 1912 and was the first chancellor of the University of Hong Kong, founded in 1911. Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> in 1912, he amalgamated the northern and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> in 1914 and was governor-general until 1919. From 1922 to 1936 he was the British member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>' Permanent Mandates Commission.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Lugard is remembered above all for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>, in which colonial officials governed through existing rulers. In The Dual Mandate in British Tropical Africa (1922) he argued that Africa could be administered both for its inhabitants' welfare and for the world's benefit, on three principles: decentralisation, continuity and cooperation. He held racist and paternalistic views, describing Africans as like children, and scholars have criticised indirect rule for building hierarchies and increasing ethnic conflict. He is also criticised for keeping the North apart from the South. He died on 11 April 1945, aged 87, and the barony he had held since 1928 became extinct.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick Lugard, 1st Baron Lugard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Local rule followed <b>Frederick Lugard</b>'s indirect rule, which worked through chiefs; they had ruled by consent and could be destooled by their elders, but under the colonial government they looked to Accra for decisions, and many came to see themselves as an aristocracy rewarded with honours and knighthoods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>During the religious wars of 1888–1892, armed Protestant, Catholic and Muslim factions fought for control of the kingdom, and in January 1892 the Battle of Mengo, in which <b>Frederick Lugard</b> of the Imperial British East Africa Company backed the Protestants with a Maxim gun, decided the issue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Frederick Lugard</b> restored Toro under Kasagama in 1891 and stationed troops along the border, and the campaign launched in 1893–94 occupied the kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bunyoro">Kingdom of Bunyoro · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The threat to its existence came with the chartering of the Royal Niger Company in 1886, whose forces attacked Bida and Ilorin in 1897, and the creation of the Protectorate of Northern Nigeria under <b>Frederick Lugard</b> on 1 January 1900.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under indirect rule, set out by <b>Frederick Lugard</b> in The Dual Mandate in British Tropical Africa (1922), existing rulers such as the emirs of the Sokoto Caliphate in Northern Nigeria kept their courts and taxes under British supervision, as the Indian princely states did under the Raj; where…</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>…lay outside Aristotle's frame altogether: government by a distant state, in pursuit of interests “often defined in a distant metropolis”, in Jürgen Osterhammel's words, over people who were subjects rather than citizens, ruled directly by colonial officials or indirectly through local rulers, as <b>Frederick Lugard</b> prescribed in 1922.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Dorking, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.08,80.28&amp;year=1914" aria-label="Chennai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6752 1676 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick Lugard"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7202 1946h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7202 1946h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.08,80.28&amp;year=1914">What was here in 1914? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
